{"reply":"You can acknowledge the effort without treating it as a reason to give up your voice. Try separating appreciation from agreement:

> “I recognize that you’ve been carrying a lot and trying to keep the project moving. I appreciate the work you’ve put in. I also want to be part of decisions that affect our work, so when plans change, could we have a chance to discuss them before they’re finalized? I’m not questioning your commitment—I’m asking for a more collaborative process.”

If you want to make it even less personal, focus on the process and the specific request: “When planning meetings end and the plan later changes, I’m unsure how to raise concerns or contribute. Could we agree on how the team will be consulted?” That lets you speak about the impact and what you need without judging their character.","memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

